{"id":"20260830T075052.842768194Z-a8533512","time":"2026-08-30T07:50:52.842768194Z","session_id":"session-20260830T072521.605271812Z","task_id":"task-1788074721605515483","source":"claude","kind":"output","summary":"{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CeYZu9MVHpAgBY969UVvP\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":538,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":104573,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":538},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a29385ef-712d-466c-af70-45dd1fbdd50a\",\"uuid\":\"9b57bf12-cef1-4b2e-a57c-649b00f8a3e5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-30T07:50:52.842Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CeYZu83cBUkhpi8SHhxjr\"}","payload":{"stream":"stdout"}}
{"id":"20260830T075109.837743258Z-2c4f01d6","time":"2026-08-30T07:51:09.837743258Z","session_id":"session-20260830T072521.605271812Z","task_id":"task-1788074721605515483","source":"claude","kind":"output","summary":"{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011CeYZu9MVHpAgBY969UVvP\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Implemented the bounded plan. Summary:\\n\\n**1. Fail-closed post-validation capture** (`internal/workflow/engine.go:1329`)\\nAfter `e.validate` returns, the run now re-captures the candidate canonically against its recorded base and the plan's declared paths (`candidate.CandidateCapture(ctx, tc.Identity.BaseSHA, tc.Files)`), and refuses unless both the returned validation diff and the evidence bundle are about that capture's canonical rendering — `certifiedAgainstCapture` (engine.go:4591) checks the bundle's own binding *and* every check inside it, so a bundle that reads as complete while one check speaks about other bytes is refused. An empty check list is not turned into a failure (a repo may configure no certifying checks), but its binding must still name this candidate's post-validation content.\\n\\n**2. Downstream identity rebound.** `capture, diff = reviewed, reviewed.Diff` — so audit, dark-run classification, review packet, `EvidenceSnapshot.ChangedPaths`, the `{B, D, T}` review binding, `noteCapturedTree`, `noteCandidateWork` and therefore the mint all use the verified post-validation diff, tree, base tree and exact path set. The mint's independent re-measurement and canonical identity checks are untouched.\\n\\n**3. Regression** (`internal/workflow/postvalidation_test.go`), driving a real `gofmt -w` that rewrites candidate content plus a `gofmt -l` verifier:\\n- `TestTheTreeAFormatterProducedIsTheTreeThatMints` — the formatter moves the tree, the post-validation capture is what validation certifies, that exact accepted state mints and reconstructs canonically, and a later worktree mutation is still refused with \\\"moved after it was reviewed\\\".\\n- `TestThePreValidationCaptureIsNotTheReviewedState` — pins the old failure: binding to the pre-validation capture refuses the accepted candidate at the mint, blaming movement that never happened.\\n- `TestValidationEvidenceAboutOtherBytesIsRefused` — the four fail-closed cases (returned diff ≠ rendering, bundle bound to other content, to another candidate, one stale check inside a well-bound bundle).\\n\\n**Judgement call worth flagging:** `validate`'s post-format re-read went through `repo.CandidateDiff` (which captures with *no* intended paths). Left alone, a plan that declares a binary/oversized output would have its own declared artifact excluded by that re-read and kept by the identity capture, producing a false refusal from the new check. I passed `tc.Files` into `validate` and re-read through `CandidateCapture(base, intended)` so both sides use one boundary. This is inside engine.go and inside step 1's verification, but the plan did not name it.\\n\\n**Also worth the architect's attention (left undone):** the new check makes it a hard refusal if a *non-mutating* certifying check (vet/build/test) actually writes a tracked file — previously that surfaced later as a mint-time movement refusal with the wrong cause. I also did not re-emit `CandidateArtifactExcluded` for artifacts the post-validation capture excludes (e.g. a binary a build check produced); the pre-validation capture's boundary report is unchanged. And I recorded no `sensei propose` scar — that stages governance YAML outside this plan.\\n\\n**Verification** (`gofmt -l cmd internal` clean, `go vet ./...` clean, `go test ./...`): 39 packages ok, 0 failures. Two `internal/publish` tests fail *only* when this session's own capability guard is on the environment (`GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0` → `core.hooksPath` refusing `git push`); with that env unset they pass. That is environmental and unrelated to this change — no code of mine touches `internal/publish`.\\n\\nAWG: briefing(internal/workflow/engine.go) | invariants: candidate identity is one post-validation measurement (B, D, T agree or the run refuses), reviewed-tree/base-parent/movement refusals preserved unchanged | uncertainty: a validation check that mutates a tracked file now refuses at validation rather than at the mint\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":538,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":104573,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":538},\"output_tokens\":2,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a29385ef-712d-466c-af70-45dd1fbdd50a\",\"uuid\":\"579357c6-d304-46ee-ad6b-b79286cd9ad9\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-30T07:51:09.824Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011CeYZu83cBUkhpi8SHhxjr\"}","payload":{"stream":"stdout"}}
